U='https://www.dot.ga.gov/PartnerSmart/Business/Source/specs/ss550.pdf'
S='Georgia DOT Supplemental Specification Section 550, Storm Drain Pipe, Pipe-Arch Culverts, and Side Drain Pipe (revised 2015; read 2026-11-20)'
add('spec-ga-pipe-1','GA','Georgia GDOT storm drain pipe: video and laser inspection, 5 and 7.5 percent deflection (Section 550)',
"Georgia (GDOT Section 550, Storm Drain Pipe, Pipe-Arch Culverts, and Side Drain Pipe, supplemental specification revised 2015). Foundation and backfill follow Section 207 and Georgia Standards 1030D and 1030P (trench width and cover are on those drawings, not in the text). Thermoplastic pipe outside the roadbed in fills up to 10 feet uses Class II B2 or better soil for HDPE and Class II B3 or better for PVC and polypropylene; above 10 feet, graded aggregate (550.3.03). Joints: concrete pipe with socket ends upstream and rubber gaskets; metal pipe with outside laps upstream and coupling bands with two or more bolts; thermoplastic joints at least silt tight (550.3.05.C). Post-installation inspection (550.3.06): once compacted backfill reaches 8 feet or final cover is complete, with at least seven days notice to the Engineer; low-distortion video with laser profiling of 100 percent of storm drain pipe and at least 10 percent of side drain pipe on state route projects, by NASSCO PACP certified operators; manual inspection is allowed in pipe over 48 inches. Deflection over 5 percent of nominal diameter needs an evaluation by a Professional Engineer; over 7.5 percent is remediated or replaced. A hand-pulled mandrel (GDT 136) can be used to confirm. Joint separation over 1 inch must be fixed. Lift thickness and compaction percent were not in this section; see Section 207.",S,U,'2015 supplemental','medium')

U='https://transportation.ky.gov/Construction/Standard%20amd%20Supplemental%20Specifications/700%20Draninage,%20Traffic%20and%20Roadside%20Construction.pdf'
S='Kentucky Transportation Cabinet Standard Specifications for Road and Bridge Construction, Division 700, Sections 701, 705, 706, as posted by KYTC (edition year not shown in the file; read 2026-11-20)'
add('spec-ky-pipe-1','KY','Kentucky KYTC culvert and storm sewer pipe: bedding, 6-inch lifts, 95 percent, 48 inches cover (Section 701)',
"Kentucky (KYTC Standard Specifications, Section 701, culvert pipe, entrance pipe and storm sewer pipe). Bedding: No. 8 or No. 9-M stone or fine aggregate; do not use DGA or gravel base (701.02.04). Backfill in 6-inch lifts compacted to not less than 95 percent of maximum density by Kentucky Method KM 64-511 (701.03.06). Keep construction equipment and traffic off the pipe until there is 48 inches of cover over it (701.03.07). Joints are soil tight and wrapped with geotextile; concrete pipe joints may be mortar (1 cement to 2 sand), asphalt mastic, rubber gaskets or butyl sealant, with tied joints within 12 feet of the outlet on slopes 3:1 or steeper; metal pipe bands are at least 12 inches wide for pipe 54 inches and smaller; thermoplastic pipe uses bell and spigot with elastomeric seals (701.03.05). Trench limits are on the Standard Drawings.",S,U,'current as posted','medium')
add('spec-ky-inspect-1','KY','Kentucky KYTC pipeline camera inspection, mandrel and deflection pay cuts (Section 705); boring and jacking (706)',
"Kentucky (KYTC Standard Specifications, Section 705, pipeline inspection). Installed pipe gets a camera inspection under Kentucky Method KM 64-114, with at least 24 hours notice to the Engineer, 30 days or more after earthwork is complete to within 1 foot of finished subgrade (705.02). Flexible pipe up to 48 inches can be checked with a mandrel that has at least 9 legs (705.03). Deflection table (705.05): 0 to 5.0 percent, full payment; 5.1 to 9.9 percent, 50 percent payment; 10 percent or greater, remove and replace. Section 706, boring and jacking: access pits on both sides, guide rails kept to line and grade, casing fitted tight to the bore with continuous welds. Minimum steel casing wall: 0.375 inch for 18 inch and smaller, 0.500 inch for 24 to 30 inch, 0.532 inch for 36 inch, 0.625 inch for 42 inch (706.02.01).",S,U,'current as posted','medium')

U='https://ia.iowadot.gov/erl/current/GS/content/2417.htm'
S='Iowa DOT Standard Specifications (current online General Supplemental series, page revised 10/20/2026), Section 2417 Corrugated Culverts (read 2026-11-20)'
add('spec-ia-culvert-1','IA','Iowa DOT corrugated metal and plastic culverts: cover and the 5 percent deflection test (Section 2417)',
"Iowa (Iowa DOT Standard Specifications, Section 2417, Corrugated Culverts, online version revised 10/20/2026). Corrugated metal or polyethylene roadway culverts get Class B bedding as shown in the contract documents (2417.03.C.1). Minimum cover: 1 foot over entrance culverts and 2 feet over roadway culverts; if construction traffic will cross, roadway culvert cover goes up to the pipe's nominal diameter or 3 feet, whichever is greater (2417.03.B). Polyethylene pipe deflection test (2417.03.C.2): at least 10 percent of locations, no sooner than 30 calendar days after backfill and before paving; the inside diameter may not shrink more than 5.0 percent; pipe 30 inches and smaller is tested with a nine-point mandrel, larger pipe is checked along its whole length. City street and utility work in Iowa usually follows SUDAS instead of the DOT book.",S,U,'2026 online')
